= Conclusion <Conclusion>

//This study compared newer concurrency mechanisms and PostgreSQL pipelining as ways to improve `pygrametl` ETL performance. The results show that these approaches address different costs. Multiprocessing and free-threading reduce wall time by distributing work across workers, while asynchronous extraction provides little improvement on its own for the workload tested. Subinterpreters have some advantages in task distribution and memory use, but their slower dictionary transfer and worker initialization do not justify replacing the existing multiprocessing implementation on the evidence available.

//The clearest improvement comes from reducing database round trips. Pipeline mode makes the sequential workflow about 3.1 times faster at the largest workload while using less CPU time. Combining pipelining with free-threaded workers gives the shortest observed execution time, about 6.5 times faster than the sequential baseline, at the cost of higher CPU use. However, segmentation faults in three free-threaded pipeline runs prevent us from treating this result as production-ready. In this workload, database pipelining is the most compelling direction; free-threading is a promising complement once its reliability is established.
